### 1. Architectural Decision: Decorator Pattern (Not a New UI Implementation)

After analyzing the codebase, the best approach is a **decorator/wrapper** that sits alongside `NonInteractiveUI` rather than a fourth `UserInterface` implementation. Here is the reasoning:

**Why not a new `UserInterface` impl?** The `UserInterface` trait has 15+ methods with complex contracts (prompts return `Result<PromptResult>`, spinners return `Box<dyn SpinnerHandle>`, etc.). A fourth implementation would duplicate significant non-GHA logic. The GHA formatting is strictly *additive* -- it emits `::error::`, `::warning::`, `::group::` annotations **in addition to** the normal non-interactive output, not instead of it.

**Why not just extend `NonInteractiveUI`?** Sprinkling `if self.is_github_actions { ... }` throughout `NonInteractiveUI` would make the code harder to reason about and test in isolation.

**Recommended approach: `GhaAnnotator` struct wrapping a `Box<dyn UserInterface>`.**  
This wrapper delegates all calls to the inner UI, and additionally emits GitHub Actions workflow commands to stdout when appropriate. It implements `UserInterface` itself, so it can be transparently swapped in at the `create_ui` call site.

For lint output, a separate `GhaFormatter` implements the existing `LintFormatter` trait (parallel to `SarifFormatter`, `JsonFormatter`, `HumanFormatter`).

### 2. Implementation Components

The plan has five components, each of which becomes a separate commit:

---

#### Component A: GitHub Actions Workflow Command Module

**New file: `src/ui/gha.rs`**

This module provides low-level functions for emitting GitHub Actions workflow commands. It encapsulates the `::` protocol so that no other code needs to construct those strings manually.

```rust
/// Emit a GitHub Actions annotation.
/// https://docs.github.com/en/actions/writing-workflows/choosing-what-your-workflow-does/workflow-commands-for-github-actions
pub fn annotation(level: GhaLevel, message: &str, properties: &GhaProperties) { ... }

pub fn group_start(title: &str) { ... }
pub fn group_end() { ... }

pub enum GhaLevel { Notice, Warning, Error }

pub struct GhaProperties {
    pub file: Option<String>,
    pub line: Option<usize>,
    pub col: Option<usize>,
    pub end_line: Option<usize>,
    pub end_col: Option<usize>,
    pub title: Option<String>,
}
```

The `annotation()` function writes to stdout in the format:
```
::error file={file},line={line},col={col},title={title}::{message}
```

Properties with `None` values are omitted from the output. The message is escaped per GHA requirements (newlines become `%0A`, `%` becomes `%25`, etc.).

**Key detail:** `group_start`/`group_end` also write to stdout, which is what GitHub Actions monitors for grouping.

**Tests:** Unit tests that capture stdout (using a `Write` trait parameter rather than direct `println!`) verify the exact format string output. Each function takes a `&mut impl Write` parameter for testability, with convenience wrappers that use stdout.

---

#### Component B: `GhaAnnotator` -- UserInterface Decorator

**New file: `src/ui/gha_annotator.rs`**

This struct wraps any `Box<dyn UserInterface>` and adds GitHub Actions annotations:

```rust
pub struct GhaAnnotator {
    inner: Box<dyn UserInterface>,
}
```

It implements `UserInterface` by delegating to `inner` for all methods, with additional behavior:

| Method | Additional GHA behavior |
|--------|------------------------|
| `error(msg)` | Emits `::error::{msg}` after delegating |
| `warning(msg)` | Emits `::warning::{msg}` after delegating |
| `show_error_block(cmd, output, hint)` | Emits `::error title=Step failed: {cmd}::{output}` after delegating |
| `show_run_summary(summary)` | For each failed step in `summary.failed_steps`, emits `::error title=Step failed::{name}` after delegating |
| `start_spinner(msg)` | Returns a `GhaSpinnerHandle` that wraps the inner spinner and emits `::group::{msg}` on creation, `::endgroup::` on finish |
| `start_spinner_indented(msg, indent)` | Same as above |
| `show_run_header(...)` | Emits `::group::Bivvy: {app_name} ({workflow})` |
| `finish_workflow_progress()` | Emits `::endgroup::` to close the outer group |

The `GhaSpinnerHandle` wraps a `Box<dyn SpinnerHandle>`:

```rust
struct GhaSpinnerHandle {
    inner: Box<dyn SpinnerHandle>,
    group_open: bool,
}

impl SpinnerHandle for GhaSpinnerHandle {
    fn finish_success(&mut self, msg: &str) {
        self.inner.finish_success(msg);
        if self.group_open {
            gha::group_end();
            self.group_open = false;
        }
    }
    fn finish_error(&mut self, msg: &str) {
        self.inner.finish_error(msg);
        if self.group_open {
            gha::group_end();
            self.group_open = false;
        }
        // Note: the ::error:: annotation is emitted by show_error_block,
        // not here, to avoid duplication
    }
    // ... delegate other methods
}
```

**Group nesting design:** Each step execution in `run_with_ui` calls `start_spinner_indented`, which opens a `::group::`. When the spinner finishes (success, error, or skip), the group is closed with `::endgroup::`. This gives GitHub Actions a collapsible log section per step. The `show_run_header` opens an outer group, and `finish_workflow_progress` (or `show_run_summary`) closes it.

**Tests:** Unit tests use `MockUI` as the inner UI and verify that both the MockUI captures and the GHA annotations are emitted. Since the GHA output goes to stdout, the test version takes a `Write` sink.

---

#### Component C: `GhaFormatter` -- Lint Output Formatter

**New file: `src/lint/output/gha.rs`**

A new `LintFormatter` implementation that formats diagnostics as GitHub Actions annotations:

```rust
pub struct GhaFormatter;

impl LintFormatter for GhaFormatter {
    fn format<W: Write>(
        &self,
        diagnostics: &[LintDiagnostic],
        writer: &mut W,
    ) -> std::io::Result<()> {
        for diag in diagnostics {
            let level = match diag.severity {
                Severity::Error => "error",
                Severity::Warning => "warning",
                Severity::Hint => "notice",
            };

            let mut props = Vec::new();
            if let Some(ref span) = diag.span {
                props.push(format!("file={}", span.file.display()));
                props.push(format!("line={}", span.start_line));
                if span.start_col > 1 {
                    props.push(format!("col={}", span.start_col));
                }
                if span.end_line != span.start_line {
                    props.push(format!("endLine={}", span.end_line));
                }
                if span.end_col < usize::MAX {
                    props.push(format!("endColumn={}", span.end_col));
                }
            }
            props.push(format!("title={}", diag.rule_id));

            let props_str = props.join(",");
            // Escape the message: % -> %25, \n -> %0A, \r -> %0D
            let message = escape_gha(&diag.message);
            writeln!(writer, "::{level} {props_str}::{message}")?;
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

This follows the same pattern as `SarifFormatter` and `JsonFormatter`. The output for a diagnostic with file location would look like:

```
::error file=.bivvy/config.yml,line=15,col=5,title=circular-dependency::Circular dependency detected: a -> b -> a
```

**Registration:** Add `"gha"` to the match in `LintCommand::format_output()` and add `GhaFormatter` to the lint module exports.

**Tests:** Follow the same pattern as `sarif.rs` tests -- create diagnostics, format them, assert on the output string.

---

#### Component D: Detection and CLI Integration

**Modifications to existing files:**

1. **`src/shell/platform.rs`**: Add a new `is_github_actions()` function:
   ```rust
   /// Check if running specifically in GitHub Actions.
   pub fn is_github_actions() -> bool {
       std::env::var("GITHUB_ACTIONS").is_ok()
   }
   ```

2. **`src/shell/mod.rs`**: Re-export `is_github_actions`.

3. **`src/cli/args.rs`**: Add a `--gha` global flag to `Cli`:
   ```rust
   /// Emit GitHub Actions workflow commands (annotations, groups)
   #[arg(long, global = true)]
   pub gha: bool,
   ```
   This provides an explicit opt-in that works even outside GitHub Actions (useful for testing).

4. **`src/ui/terminal.rs`**: Modify `create_ui` to accept a `gha` parameter and wrap the UI:
   ```rust
   pub fn create_ui(interactive: bool, mode: OutputMode, gha: bool) -> Box<dyn UserInterface> {
       let base: Box<dyn UserInterface> = if interactive && Term::stdout().is_term() {
           Box::new(TerminalUI::new(mode))
       } else {
           Box::new(NonInteractiveUI::new(mode))
       };

       if gha {
           Box::new(GhaAnnotator::new(base))
       } else {
           base
       }
   }
   ```

5. **`src/main.rs`**: Determine GHA mode and pass it:
   ```rust
   let gha_mode = cli.gha || is_github_actions();
   let mut ui = create_ui(is_interactive, output_mode, gha_mode);
   ```

6. **`src/cli/commands/lint.rs`**: Add `"gha"` to the format match in `format_output()`:
   ```rust
   "gha" => {
       let formatter = GhaFormatter;
       formatter.format(diagnostics, &mut output).ok();
   }
   ```
   Also update the auto-detection: when `self.args.format == "human"` and GHA is detected, automatically switch to emitting both human-readable and GHA annotations. Alternatively (simpler), users would pass `--format gha` explicitly, or the `GhaAnnotator` wrapper would handle run-time errors.

7. **`src/lint/output/mod.rs`**: Add `gha` module and `Gha` variant to `OutputFormat`:
   ```rust
   pub mod gha;
   pub use gha::GhaFormatter;

   pub enum OutputFormat {
       Human,
       Json,
       Sarif,
       Gha,
   }
   ```

8. **`src/lint/mod.rs`**: Re-export `GhaFormatter`.

---

#### Component E: `--format gha` Auto-Detection for Lint

When running `bivvy lint` inside GitHub Actions without explicitly passing `--format gha`, there is a question of whether to auto-switch the format. The recommendation is:

- **Do not auto-switch `--format`** for lint. The user explicitly chooses their lint format. Instead, when GHA mode is active (via `--gha` flag or auto-detection), the `GhaAnnotator` wrapper will call `ui.error()` for error-level diagnostics emitted through the human formatter path, and those `error()` calls will trigger `::error::` annotations automatically.
- **Provide `--format gha`** as an explicit option for users who want *only* GHA annotations (no human-readable output). This is useful in CI pipelines where the human output is noise and only annotations matter.

This means there are two valid CI workflows:
1. `bivvy lint` (default) -- human-readable output + automatic GHA `::error::` annotations via the `GhaAnnotator` wrapper
2. `bivvy lint --format gha` -- only GHA workflow command output, no human formatting

---

